.TH AT 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
at \- schedule a command for later execution
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B at
.RB [ \-m ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-q
.IR queue ]
.B \-t
.I time
.br
.B at
.RB [ \-m ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-q
.IR queue ]
.I timespec
.RI [ timespec ]\ .\|.\|.
.br
.B at
.B \-r
.I job_id
.RI [ job_id ]\ .\|.\|.
.br
.B at
.B \-l
.B \-q
.I queue
.br
.B at
.B \-l
[\fIjob_id\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B at
reads a shell command script from
.I file
(if
.B \-f
is given) or from standard input, and submits it as a job to be run once,
at a later time computed from either
.B \-t
.I time
or a
.I timespec
operand sequence. Submission only queues the job: it is actually executed
later, out of process, by the
.BR atd (1)
daemon, which must be running for any submitted job to ever run.
.PP
On successful submission, a decimal job identifier and the resolved run
time are printed to standard error as
"\fBjob\fR \fIid\fR \fBat\fR \fItime\fR".
.PP
.B at
.B \-l
lists pending jobs (all of them, or only the ones named by
.I job_id
operands, or only those in a given
.BR \-q ) .
Each listed job is printed as its id, a tab, and the job's scheduled run
time (and, if it is not in the default queue, the queue letter).
.PP
.B at
.B \-r
removes the named jobs from the spool; a job already claimed or run by
.B atd
can still be removed, but has no further effect.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-m
Accepted for compatibility (real
.BR at (1p)
uses this to request mail notification of a job's output on completion).
Has no effect on this implementation's behavior: every job's captured
output is retained regardless of whether
.B \-m
is given, since there is no mail transport to make a mail-versus-discard
distinction meaningful.
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Read the job's command script from
.I file
instead of standard input.
.TP
.BI \-q " queue"
Submit the job to (or, with
.BR \-l ", list only jobs in)"
queue
.IR queue ,
a single letter. Only the first character of
.I queue
is significant. If omitted, or given as an empty string, the job is
submitted to queue
.RB \` a \' .
Queue letters are recorded and reported back by
.B \-l
but otherwise do not affect how or when a job runs, with one exception:
a job submitted to queue
.RB \` b \'
(the queue
.BR batch (1)
itself submits to) is deferred by
.B atd
while system load is high, in keeping with
.BR batch (1p) 's
own "run when the system is not too busy" behavior.
.IP
When
.B \-l
is combined with one or more
.I job_id
operands,
.B \-q
is ignored: the named jobs are always listed regardless of their queue.
.TP
.BI \-t " time"
Submit the job to run at the given absolute
.IR time ,
in the same
\fI[[CC]YY]MMDDhhmm[.SS]\fR
format
.BR touch (1)'s
own
.B \-t
option accepts (all-numeric fields, two digits each except the optional
leading century/year prefix; see
.BR touch (1)
for the full field breakdown). Mutually exclusive with a
.I timespec
operand sequence.
.TP
.B \-l
List pending jobs instead of submitting one.
.TP
.B \-r
Remove the named jobs instead of submitting one.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I timespec
One or more shell words forming a time specification, used in place of
.B \-t
.IR time .
The recognized grammar is:
.RS
.PP
.nf
timespec  := nowspec | time [date] [increment]
nowspec   := "now" [increment]
time      := hr24clock_hr_min | hr24clock_hour ':' minute
           | wallclock_hr_min am_pm | wallclock_hour ':' minute am_pm
           | "noon" | "midnight"
date      := month_name day_number [',' year_number]
           | day_of_week | "today" | "tomorrow" | iso_date
increment := '+' number period | "next" period
period    := "minute(s)" | "hour(s)" | "day(s)" | "week(s)"
           | "month(s)" | "year(s)"
.fi
.RE
.IP
.I month_name
and
.I day_of_week
are matched case-insensitively against their first three letters (e.g.
.RB \` sep \'
or
.RB \` September \'
both match September).
.I iso_date
is an extension beyond the standard grammar: a bare
\fICCYY\-MM\-DD\fR
date, accepted anywhere
.I date
is, and also accepted completely on its own with no leading
.I time
word, in which case the time of day defaults to midnight.
.I timezone_name
(a trailing word after
.IR time ,
per the standard grammar) is not implemented: a trailing word that is
not a valid
.IR date ,
.IR increment ,
or the end of the operand list is a parse failure, since this
implementation has no time zone name database to resolve it against.
.IP
A bare
.I time
with no
.I date
rolls forward to tomorrow if that time of day has already passed today.
A
.I day_of_week
with no explicit time correction rolls forward a week at a time until
the result is in the future. A
.I month_name day_number
with no
.I year_number
rolls the year forward by one if that date has already passed this
year.
.RB \` today \' ,
.RB \` tomorrow \' ,
and the
.I iso_date
form never roll forward this way: whatever date is named is what gets
scheduled, even if it names a time already in the past.
.IP
Because
.I timespec
words are ordinary shell arguments and this utility's own option
parsing stops at the first word beginning with
.B \-
that it does not recognize, a
.I timespec
word must not itself begin with
.BR \- ;
use
.B \-\-
to end option parsing first if that is unavoidable.
.TP
.I job_id
A decimal job identifier, as printed at submission time or by
.BR \-l .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The job was successfully submitted, removed, or listed.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred: an invalid or incomplete option, a missing or
unparsable time specification, both a
.I timespec
and
.B \-t
given together, extra operands after
.BR \-t ,
a named
.I job_id
that does not exist, or a failure accessing the job spool.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR atd (1),
.BR batch (1),
.BR crontab (1),
.BR crond (1),
.BR touch (1)
